<!-- SOLVENCY VS NON-SOLVENCY PROPELLANTS -->
<section class="section" id="solvency-vs-non-solvency-propellants">
  <h2>Solvency vs Non-Solvency Propellants</h2>
  <p>Solvency refers to a propellant’s ability to dissolve or partially dissolve formulation components such
                as resins, oils, or active ingredients. This property affects formulation simplicity, phase stability,
                and packaging compatibility.</p>
            <p>Solvent behavior must be aligned with formulation goals. Improper use can cause swelling of seals,
                extraction of elastomers, or destabilization of concentrates. Physical and solvency property data for
                individual propellants is documented in <a href="https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov">PubChem</a>. See our
                dedicated guide to <a href="/dimethyl-ether-dme-aerosol-propellant-guide/">DME as an aerosol propellant</a>
                for its solvency parameters and valve elastomer compatibility in more depth.</p>
            <table>
                <tr>
