<!-- APPLICATION-BASED PROPELLANT SELECTION -->
<section class="section" id="application-based-propellant-selection">
  <h2>Application-Based Propellant Selection</h2>
  <p>Propellant choice must align with end-use requirements rather than general performance metrics.
                Industrial coatings, personal care products, and technical aerosols impose different demands on spray
                behavior and formulation compatibility. Regulatory pressure to reduce global warming potential is
                driving adoption of low-GWP alternatives tracked by the
                <a href="https://ozone.unep.org">UNEP Ozone Secretariat</a> under the Kigali Amendment — see our guide
                to <a href="/hfo-1234ze-propellant-low-gwp-alternative/">HFO-1234ze as a low-GWP propellant</a> for one
                such alternative gaining traction in personal care and pharmaceutical aerosols.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Paints and coatings: LPG or LPG/DME blends</li>
                <li>Cleaners and degreasers: LPG or CO₂</li>
                <li>Personal care products: DME or HFCs</li>
                <li>Technical aerosols: N₂ or CO₂</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
</section>

<!-- COMMON PERFORMANCE FAILURES LINKED TO PROPELLANT CHOICE -->
<section class="section" id="common-performance-failures-linked-to-propellant-choice">
  <h2>Common Performance Failures Linked to Propellant Choice</h2>
  <p>Many aerosol failures originate from mismatches between propellant physics and product design. These
                issues are often misattributed to valves or formulations when the root cause is thermodynamic.
                Flammability risk in particular is governed by the propellant's flash point — see our explainer on
                <a href="/flash-point-explained/">why flash point matters in aerosol safety</a> for how this single
                property drives flammability classification. Storage classification for flammable propellant blends
                follows <a href="https://www.nfpa.org">NFPA 30B</a>.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Pressure drop before can is empty</li>
                <li>Weak spray in cold conditions</li>
                <li>Phase separation over shelf life</li>
                <li>Excessive flammability risk</li>
                <li>Inconsistent spray pattern</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

