</section>

<!-- ENGINEERING THE SPRAY, NOT JUST FILLING A CAN -->
<section class="section" id="engineering-the-spray-not-just-filling-a-can">
  <h2>Engineering the Spray, Not Just Filling a Can</h2>
  <p>An aerosol product is a <strong>closed thermodynamic system</strong>, not just a container for chemicals.
                Propellant selection determines whether the system behaves predictably across temperature, usage rate,
                and storage conditions.</p>
            <p>Because vapor pressure, solvency, and flammability are properties of the propellant itself rather than
                the formulation around it, no amount of downstream adjustment to the actuator, valve, or fragrance load
                can fully compensate for a propellant mismatched to the product's performance requirements. Treating
                propellant selection as an early, deliberate engineering decision — rather than a late-stage sourcing
                choice — is what separates a product that performs consistently for its full shelf life from one that
                weakens, separates, or fails a burst test after the fact.</p>
        </section>

